Market Challenges
Taste Profile and Consumer Acceptance
Certain stevia extracts can produce a lingering bitter or licorice-like aftertaste, which may affect consumer acceptance in products designed to replicate the taste of sugar. Manufacturers continue to invest in taste-masking technologies, blending strategies, and improved glycoside formulations to address this challenge.

Supply Chain and Cost Volatility
Stevia cultivation is sensitive to climatic conditions, and variations in agricultural yields can affect raw leaf availability and pricing. Multi-stage extraction and purification processes also increase production costs, particularly for high-purity glycosides.

Market Restraints
Competition from artificial sweeteners such as aspartame and sucralose, as well as natural alternatives such as monk fruit and allulose, can limit stevia's market penetration. Differences in regulatory requirements, permissible intake levels, and labeling rules across countries may also complicate international product launches. High investment requirements for extraction and purification facilities create additional barriers for smaller manufacturers.

